"""Roadmap 79 stays OPEN, measured, with a facade planner proposed (docs/findings/facade_census_79/,
docs/proposals/FACADE_PLANNER.md).

Anchored: the item's whole status block and the end of its body, read verbatim from the roadmap when this was
written, must each be there exactly once; nothing is written otherwise. Every line LF. Then `python
tools/roadmap_status.py --write` and `--check` regenerate and verify the index.

    python patches/patch_roadmap_79_census.py
"""
import pathlib
import sys

ROADMAP = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / "PIPELINE_ROADMAP.md"
EDITS = [("roadmap 79's status", '*STATUS: OPEN 2026-09-06 -- INDEPENDENTLY NAMED A SECOND TIME, FROM THE ART\nSIDE, AND THE TWO DESCRIPTIONS AGREE. `pixelcoat/docs/CONTRAST_DIRECTION.md`\n§6.2 reaches this item\'s conclusion from a measurement rather than from a\nwalkthrough: "Pixelcoat generates fields; Quake II textures are compositions",\nand "a tiling field generator cannot produce one no matter how good the\ngenerator gets. The missing subsystem is not a better field -- it is a PANEL\nCOMPOSER that takes a quiet field from an existing grammar and lays graphic\nelements onto it at authored positions." That is this item\'s "the object that\nwould carry a composition does not exist", said in Pixelcoat\'s own vocabulary.\n§6.1 adds the measurement that explains WHY the buildings read flat even\nthough the palette is disciplined: a painted bevel needs SEPARATED value\nclusters -- bright edge, mid field, dark band, near-black interior, highlight,\nwith gaps between them -- and 22 of 51 grammars have all their value mass in a\nsingle contiguous run. The doc\'s phrase for it is "the palette has no depth\nvocabulary". CONFIRMED THE HARD WAY 2026-09-06: two concrete grammars were\nauthored against this item using the existing field generator\'s `form_lines`\nprimitive, and the walk verdict on the better of them was that it "reads as\nstripes, not intentional architecture". A field generator emitting regular\nseams is not a composition, which is exactly what §6.2 predicts. WHOEVER PICKS\nTHIS UP should read that document first -- it carries the measurements, and\nthe two halves of the answer it states ("quiet at the macro level, intentional\nat the texture level") are the design intent this item is serving. Original\nstatus below.*', '*STATUS: OPEN 2026-10-20 -- MEASURED, AND A PLANNER PROPOSED (`docs/findings/facade_census_79/`, `docs/proposals/FACADE_PLANNER.md`). On the 102 buildings a themed lot draws, read off their built slots: of the 169 first-storey openings in the 52 multi-storey buildings, 28 stand over a ground-floor opening, where the Empties -- whose presets lay every storey on one list of bays -- read 92.9 % through the same instrument, its control; 31 of the 39 doors tagged main or front are wider than every other exterior door, and over the 23 with a storey above, 2 have an opening; 8.2 % of solid wall is tiling remainder, in 69 widths. Moving only the 170 untagged upper windows cannot compose a face (`predict.py`): equal centred bays drop stacking over the ground floor to 5, and the best grid through the ground floor\'s own openings reaches 139 by moving windows a median 2.5 m and up to 22 m, 8 into another room. So the grid has to be chosen where the openings are first chosen, the spec\'s authoring, and the proposal puts the planner there: `facade_plan.py` in Deli Counter, a grammar table whose first rows come from the walker\'s references, its first users the two Empty families, byte-identical. In delco the joints are not what reads: the wall draws no relief (Zoo 0.51.0, `reveal` 0). Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-09-06 -- INDEPENDENTLY NAMED A SECOND TIME, FROM THE ART\nSIDE, AND THE TWO DESCRIPTIONS AGREE. `pixelcoat/docs/CONTRAST_DIRECTION.md`\n§6.2 reaches this item\'s conclusion from a measurement rather than from a\nwalkthrough: "Pixelcoat generates fields; Quake II textures are compositions",\nand "a tiling field generator cannot produce one no matter how good the\ngenerator gets. The missing subsystem is not a better field -- it is a PANEL\nCOMPOSER that takes a quiet field from an existing grammar and lays graphic\nelements onto it at authored positions." That is this item\'s "the object that\nwould carry a composition does not exist", said in Pixelcoat\'s own vocabulary.\n§6.1 adds the measurement that explains WHY the buildings read flat even\nthough the palette is disciplined: a painted bevel needs SEPARATED value\nclusters -- bright edge, mid field, dark band, near-black interior, highlight,\nwith gaps between them -- and 22 of 51 grammars have all their value mass in a\nsingle contiguous run. The doc\'s phrase for it is "the palette has no depth\nvocabulary". CONFIRMED THE HARD WAY 2026-09-06: two concrete grammars were\nauthored against this item using the existing field generator\'s `form_lines`\nprimitive, and the walk verdict on the better of them was that it "reads as\nstripes, not intentional architecture". A field generator emitting regular\nseams is not a composition, which is exactly what §6.2 predicts. WHOEVER PICKS\nTHIS UP should read that document first -- it carries the measurements, and\nthe two halves of the answer it states ("quiet at the macro level, intentional\nat the texture level") are the design intent this item is serving. Original\nstatus below.*'), ("the end of roadmap 79's body", 'wants -- is a design decision and does not belong in a roadmap item written by\nthe person who could not see the problem until it was pointed out. This item\nowns the MECHANISM: that there is nowhere to put a grammar. Evidence:\n`_runs/walk_wear0`, and the three walkthrough screenshots of 2026-08-29.\n', "wants -- is a design decision and does not belong in a roadmap item written by\nthe person who could not see the problem until it was pointed out. This item\nowns the MECHANISM: that there is nowhere to put a grammar. Evidence:\n`_runs/walk_wear0`, and the three walkthrough screenshots of 2026-08-29.\n\n**MEASURED, 2026-10-20** (`docs/findings/facade_census_79/`: `census.py` reads the built library's slots, `predict.py` prices four candidate grammars before anything is built). The critique's first bullet has a number now -- 28 of 169 first-storey openings over a ground-floor one, against the Empties' 92.9 % -- and so does the reason an emitter-side fix cannot reach it: holding the ground floor where gameplay put it, equal bays upstairs move windows under a metre and stack 5; a grid through the ground floor's openings stacks 139 and moves windows up to 22 m. The two were laid out independently. Within one storey the openings are already evenly spaced (43 of 62 runs). `docs/proposals/FACADE_PLANNER.md` owns the mechanism this item asks for and leaves the grammar to its rows' sources.\n")]


def main():
    raw = ROADMAP.read_bytes()
    if b"\r\n" in raw:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the roadmap has CRLF lines; nothing written")
    text = raw.decode("utf-8")
    for what, old, new in EDITS:
        if text.count(old) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %s is there %d times, not once; nothing written" % (what, text.count(old)))
    for what, old, new in EDITS:
        text = text.replace(old, new)
    ROADMAP.write_bytes(text.encode("utf-8"))
    print("roadmap 79 updated; now tools/roadmap_status.py --write and --check")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
